#open-question(title: [Can a Submaximal Grip Protocol Measure Fatigability in Severe and Very Severe Patients?])[
Every published HGS cohort is ambulatory. Severe and very severe patients — housebound or bedbound, often unable to sit upright for an hour — were not represented, and the standard protocol (ten maximal grips, a 60-minute wait, ten more) is likely to exceed their capacity.

A fixed-submaximal-load protocol is the obvious alternative: squeeze to a target percentage of a measured (or assumed) maximum and hold, or perform a small number of low-effort contractions, with fatigability defined as the decline across trials rather than as a maximal-force deficit. Portable dynamometers can do this at home.

The obstacles are not trivial. Without a true maximal reference, "submaximal" is undefined; patients with post-exertional malaise may still be provoked by repeated effort; and the measurement properties of low-effort protocols in this population are unknown. Any such protocol therefore needs a safety-first design: a single contraction first, symptom monitoring for 72 hours after, and a stopping rule that does not require the patient to push through worsening.

*Prediction:* a fixed low-load protocol will be feasible in at least 80% of severe patients without a 72-hour worsening, and will show a larger between-session decline than in matched sedentary controls. *Falsified if:* the protocol cannot be completed, or the decline does not differ from controls.

*Certainty: 0.20.* (No data in severe ME/CFS; inference from the ambulatory protocols and from pacing principles.)

*Consequence:* If it works, the patients with the least access to objective assessment — the bedbound — would get a measurement they can do at home; if it does not, the paper must say plainly that this group remains unmeasured.
] <oq:handgrip-severe-submax-protocol>
